WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.402
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.830
STEPHANIE WONG: Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.450
Welcome back to
"Eyes on Enterprise,"

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.000
where I bring in Googlers
to talk about the technology

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.820
landscapes that are shaping
how enterprises adapt,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:23.500
modernize, and scale.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.120
I'm Stephanie Wong,
and today, I'm

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:27.430
bringing back on Brian Dorsey.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:29.723
He is a developer
advocate for Compute,

00:00:29.723 --> 00:00:32.140
and we're going to be talking
about how you should rethink

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.180
your VMs, which I'm
excited to hear about,

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.420
because I'm unsure of what
you actually mean by that.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.420
BRIAN DORSEY: Excellent.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:38.087
Let's do it.

00:00:38.087 --> 00:00:40.420
STEPHANIE WONG: Let's start
by examining our assumptions

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.230
about VMs.

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:42.730
What are some of
those assumptions?

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.170
To me, a VM is a
virtual computer.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.400
You have CPU, memory,
disks, networking.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:49.978
What else is different here?

00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:51.520
BRIAN DORSEY: Yes,
that's exactly it.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.440
It's relevant, though,
that it's the base of many,

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.910
many of our other services.

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:00.770
It is super common
in architectures.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:03.700
Everybody's running some VMs.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.280
But if you're only thinking
about it as a classic VM,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.458
you might be leaving time
and money on the table.

00:01:11.458 --> 00:01:12.250
STEPHANIE WONG: Oh.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:13.018
Time and money.

00:01:13.018 --> 00:01:14.560
That's everyone's
top-of-mind, right?

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.460
BRIAN DORSEY: Yep.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.252
STEPHANIE WONG: Interested
in hearing more,

00:01:17.252 --> 00:01:20.410
but as I imagine, don't
Google Cloud VMs typically fit

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:21.880
in that model?

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.000
BRIAN DORSEY: At
a high level, yes.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.623
It is an abstraction of
this CPU network disk RAM.

00:01:28.623 --> 00:01:30.040
But we're used to
thinking of that

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.530
as a slice of one
computer, and it's really

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.650
a slice of resources that are
across the whole data center.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:38.470
That enables a whole bunch
of agility and reliability

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.325
that you wouldn't notice
otherwise or you might not get.

00:01:41.325 --> 00:01:43.450
STEPHANIE WONG: I imagine
this is the basis of what

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:44.820
you mean by rethinking VMs--

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.870
slice of a data center.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:48.880
Can you dive into
more about how that's

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:50.172
truly different than otherwise?

00:01:50.172 --> 00:01:51.310
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:55.450
An example of that is,
when you buy a computer,

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:56.410
it's a big decision.

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.430
You buy it, and you have
it three to five years,

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:02.200
or whatever, and you're
stuck with that shape.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.760
When you turn on
a VM in the cloud,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:06.130
you're really setting the
configuration for that machine

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:07.270
right then.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.620
And if you turn it off, you can
keep the disk around, the boot

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:13.240
disk, and then you can just turn
it back on with a completely

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.380
different configuration.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.547
So you can be like, oh,
I've got a two-core machine.

00:02:16.547 --> 00:02:20.470
Or, now I've got a 25-core
machine or a 250-core machine,

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.740
and maybe I only want
that for an hour or a day.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.130
That kind of thinking gives
you a bunch of flexibility

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.170
to be able to respond
to peak loads,

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:31.850
to do reports that
need a big crunch

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:33.010
at the end of the month--

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.260
whatever it is, without
building a lot more complexity

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:35.950
into the system.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.658
STEPHANIE WONG: Google
is a huge company,

00:02:37.658 --> 00:02:40.190
and we have a large amount
of Compute power in the data

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:40.690
center.

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.255
What's possible with this
amount of infrastructure?

00:02:44.255 --> 00:02:45.880
BRIAN DORSEY: I think
the classic thing

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:48.040
that people talk about
is scaling out, right?

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.390
You've got one service.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.980
Then you run it on
multiple machines,

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.240
and you can scale
out really well.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:52.750
That's true.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.430
But also, I think things have
snuck up on us a little bit.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.870
The level that you can
scale up to has gotten huge.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:08.320
Our largest VMs are,
currently, 416-core machines

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.182
with 12 terabytes of
memory, large disks,

00:03:12.182 --> 00:03:13.140
and that sort of thing.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.620
So the limit that you can
scale a given machine up to,

00:03:17.620 --> 00:03:21.520
like a database, is
quite high, and if you

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:25.325
do keep it to one machine,
you simplify things a bit.

00:03:25.325 --> 00:03:26.950
I was shopping for
hard disks recently,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.450
and the largest one I could
find was 14 terabytes.

00:03:31.450 --> 00:03:35.770
You can get a machine with
almost that much memory

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:40.960
as you'd normally buy for
disks, so there's plenty of room

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:42.040
to scale up as well.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.312
And for apps that have
been around for a while,

00:03:44.312 --> 00:03:46.270
scaling out could mean
a whole re-architecture.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.020
Or scaling up, you
could just turn a dial,

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:49.937
and it can really help
solve a lot of problems

00:03:49.937 --> 00:03:51.190
and save time and money.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:52.940
STEPHANIE WONG: Why
does scale-up actually

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.190
matter in this case?

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:55.982
BRIAN DORSEY: Some of
the concrete examples

00:03:55.982 --> 00:03:58.750
are, like, a database, which
can get really complex when you

00:03:58.750 --> 00:04:01.850
start trying to [? short ?] it.

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.810
Another common use case
for the really big machines

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:08.560
is things like SAP HANA which
is a large in-memory database.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.382
It just has to run
on one machine.

00:04:12.382 --> 00:04:13.090
Things like that.

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:16.560
I think it just gives you more
headroom to keep things running

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.700
that you don't--

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:20.640
it's not the time to do
a big re-architecture

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.020
for the organization,
but it does need

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:24.610
to grow with your organization.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:25.932
You can do that more easily.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:27.640
STEPHANIE WONG: You
mentioned VM reboots.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:30.610
How fast is that,
actually, on Google Cloud?

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.560
BRIAN DORSEY: It's
actually pretty quick.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.000
We can start a machine
from scratch, boot the OS,

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.380
get to your code running
in 20 to 30 seconds.

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:44.090
For a lot of situations,
that is plenty fast.

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:45.250
And that's from nothing.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:48.682
There's no planning, no
talking to another team

00:04:48.682 --> 00:04:49.390
for provisioning.

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.480
It's just ready to go.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:51.940
STEPHANIE WONG: I've
worked with VMs before,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.130
and that usually involved
deploying the hypervisor

00:04:54.130 --> 00:04:56.560
and deploying the VM on
VirtualBox or VMware,

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.470
for example--

00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:58.700
a lot of steps, I concur.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.568
So is it easier
here, in this case?

00:05:00.568 --> 00:05:01.360
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.490
I think the main
thing is not worrying

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.150
about management of all of
the machines underneath.

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:11.890
You actually don't have to worry
about the Tetris game of trying

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:13.990
to fit your VMs in with
the right other VMs,

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:16.000
or whether you have
space for them.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.020
That sort of thing.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.610
That still happens, but
it'll happen on the back end.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:19.870
You don't have to
worry about it.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.550
STEPHANIE WONG: What does
this all mean, practically?

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:24.070
BRIAN DORSEY: You can make
the machine that's exactly

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.660
the right shape
for your workload

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.880
and not leave idle
machines running,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:30.880
because they cost money.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.520
You can actually make
it the right shape.

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:36.650
The other place
this plays out is--

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.428
when you're running
all your own stuff,

00:05:38.428 --> 00:05:39.970
you're always trying
to keep the most

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:41.730
utilization of the
hardware you can,

00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:42.920
because you've paid for it.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:45.400
You want to use it, right?

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.163
If you can make a
machine that is exactly

00:05:47.163 --> 00:05:48.580
the right shape
for your workload,

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.970
you automatically get
the right utilization.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:54.580
In fact, we'll even recommend--
oh, hey, this machine

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:56.660
isn't getting as much
use as it could be.

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.720
You might want to
make it smaller.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:05:59.380
Or vice versa.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:00.478
It seems to be slammed.

00:06:00.478 --> 00:06:02.020
Maybe it needs to
be a little bigger.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:02.710
That sort of thing.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:02.920
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:05.128
You're moving away from the
mental model of "How do I

00:06:05.128 --> 00:06:07.570
make the most use of this
box that I've already

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:10.000
deployed, procured, and
paid for" versus, "OK.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.500
I've deployed it,
but I can actually

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.690
backtrack and redeploy
using a different shape,

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:16.360
so I can optimize for the
CPU utilization, memory,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.937
and all these other things."

00:06:17.937 --> 00:06:20.020
BRIAN DORSEY: That's true
both with the planning--

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:23.200
you don't have to overcommit
early on in a project.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.757
But I think, again, where
it's not so obvious,

00:06:25.757 --> 00:06:27.340
is that time scales
can be short, too,

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:30.580
so you can reboot it and run
it for an hour in the bigger

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.202
shape, or a certain time of
the month, or what have you.

00:06:33.202 --> 00:06:34.910
STEPHANIE WONG: We
talked about scale-up.

00:06:34.910 --> 00:06:36.150
What about scale-out cases?

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:38.650
BRIAN DORSEY: This is like the
classic sweet spot for Cloud,

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.580
where you have multiple
computers cooperating

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:42.550
to do things.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:46.180
We have tools built
in to Compute Engine

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:47.450
to help you scale up and down.

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:49.270
There's a thing called
managed instance groups that

00:06:49.270 --> 00:06:51.853
connects to a load balancer, and
you can, either automatically

00:06:51.853 --> 00:06:55.900
or on your config, decide how
many machines are actually

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:57.010
behind that load balancer.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:59.860
It can be one, or it can be
20, and that can automatically

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:03.580
scale up or down
or be on a dial.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:06.160
You basically call an
API for how big or small

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:06.910
you want it to be.

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:08.500
In addition to that,
there are cases

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:10.390
where you want to be
doing batch workloads

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:12.040
or where those
machines don't all

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:15.010
have to be running that
much, and it really

00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:18.220
helps us out, for planning,
if we can schedule things.

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.470
So we offer a very
large discount

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:22.610
on machines that
don't live that long.

00:07:22.610 --> 00:07:24.520
They're called preemptible VMs.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.370
Basically, these VMs can
never live more than 24 hours

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:32.530
per machine, but they are an
80% discount in many cases,

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:35.857
so you can be looking at a fifth
of the costs of another thing.

00:07:35.857 --> 00:07:37.690
If you've got a batch
workload, or if you've

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:41.320
got a system with very little
state on the particular worker

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.335
machines, you can save
a lot of money that way.

00:07:43.335 --> 00:07:45.460
STEPHANIE WONG: Let's talk
about access to the VMs.

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:48.190
Previously, I remember
having to SSH into VMs or RDP

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:49.990
into a Windows
machine, and then I'd

00:07:49.990 --> 00:07:52.750
have to save my
session login info

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.540
and target the public
IP address of the VM.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.240
There are multiple steps, and it
wasn't always straightforward.

00:07:58.240 --> 00:07:59.500
Is this the same
with Google Cloud?

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:01.330
Are there easier ways
to access your VMs here?

00:08:01.330 --> 00:08:02.310
BRIAN DORSEY: Yeah,
or maybe you create

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.500
a jump box that's behind
the firewall you VPN into,

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:06.342
or something like that, right?

00:08:06.342 --> 00:08:08.550
If you need to, you can
still do all of those things.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:09.710
It's very flexible.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:12.530
And if you've got processes in
place, you can keep doing them.

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:13.947
But we also have
a couple of tools

00:08:13.947 --> 00:08:16.000
to make things easier than that.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.440
When you're using
the web console,

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.990
you're already logged-in and
authenticated with Google.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:20.740
You've got a user.

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:24.460
We know who you are and that
you have access to that project.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:27.100
There's actually a web-based
command line console.

00:08:27.100 --> 00:08:29.350
You can just click
a button on any VM,

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:31.450
and it will send an
SSH key back and forth,

00:08:31.450 --> 00:08:32.590
and you can log right in.

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:33.465
STEPHANIE WONG: Nice.

00:08:33.465 --> 00:08:36.580
BRIAN DORSEY: That's super
handy for one-off situations,

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:41.380
emergency things, or on a tablet
when you're out, or whatever.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.049
On the other hand,
there is a strong need

00:08:44.049 --> 00:08:47.440
in large organizations to
keep careful track of who's

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:51.280
allowed to log in where
and automate that.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.820
There's a system called OS
Login that basically delegates

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:58.660
the "who's allowed" part of
it into the overall permission

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.717
structure of Google Cloud,
which is called IAM.

00:09:02.717 --> 00:09:04.300
You can basically
delegate that there.

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:05.980
Users can pick
their own SSH key,

00:09:05.980 --> 00:09:07.720
but the central group
can decide who's

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:10.180
actually allowed to log in
to which boxes with that key.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.890
STEPHANIE WONG: Is
this making it easier

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.200
for administrators,
who are always

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.090
trying to manage
public-private key pairings

00:09:16.090 --> 00:09:18.593
and sharing it out
with their teams?

00:09:18.593 --> 00:09:20.260
How does it make it
a little bit easier?

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:23.050
BRIAN DORSEY: You can just avoid
a bunch of potential mistakes

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.950
or grunt work of dealing
with all of those,

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:30.090
and I think, on top of that,
you can audit it better.

00:09:30.090 --> 00:09:32.750
You can tell exactly what
the current configuration is,

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.090
or you can change it on the fly.

00:09:34.090 --> 00:09:35.320
STEPHANIE WONG: Let's
talk about disks.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:38.020
When I think of disks, I
imagine you have a boot volume.

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:40.158
You have additional
volumes for more storage.

00:09:40.158 --> 00:09:41.950
Is that how they work
on Google Cloud, too,

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:43.550
or different in this case?

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:46.050
BRIAN DORSEY: This is another
example of "yes, and more."

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:46.890
STEPHANIE WONG: "Yes and."

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:47.630
BRIAN DORSEY: "Yes and."

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:48.470
Your model's right.

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:50.740
You've got these disks that
you can attach or detach

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:54.210
between multiple machines.

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.710
But they are, again, a
slice of a data center.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:58.900
The disks are
actually, by default,

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:00.850
network-attached
storage, but they're not

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:03.190
attached to some particular
machine out there.

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:06.190
They're attached to hundreds
or thousands of machines

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:07.220
to get the data back.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.820
So you get very
consistent performance,

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:14.440
you get a lot of flexibility
to change things very quickly,

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:17.230
and it enables features like--

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:20.050
if your disk fills up, you
can just make it bigger.

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:24.790
It also enables things that
are really not obvious.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:29.110
Normally, a disk is in a
particular physical location.

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.390
Since these are
already spread out,

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.370
we've got a mode
where we actually

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:38.170
spread the blocks across two
zones or across a whole region.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:41.020
You can have what looks
like one disk to the VM

00:10:41.020 --> 00:10:43.180
actually living in two
physical locations,

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.870
so you get a whole bunch
of disaster recovery crisis

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:49.910
mitigation without actually
changing the app at all.

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:53.805
Essentially, what you get
is the 80-20 of a SAN.

00:10:53.805 --> 00:10:55.180
You get a lot of
the features you

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:58.900
want out of a SAN-based
disk, but in the cloud.

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.510
The other thing that I think is
maybe not so obvious is that--

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:03.343
we think of a computer
as this physical box,

00:11:03.343 --> 00:11:07.223
but in our Cloud VMs, the
disk is really the identity.

00:11:07.223 --> 00:11:09.640
A boot disk has the operating
system and all your software

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:11.770
and everything, and
the computer part

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:14.428
is what happens to be
configured currently.

00:11:14.428 --> 00:11:16.345
When you turn it off,
that part all goes away.

00:11:16.345 --> 00:11:18.678
And when you turn it back on,
we give you a new computer

00:11:18.678 --> 00:11:20.350
off the same disk.

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.640
That changes the way you think
about reliability, resiliency,

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:26.320
flexibility, that sort of thing.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.278
STEPHANIE WONG: You can
have persistent storage

00:11:28.278 --> 00:11:30.880
for whatever operating system
and files that you already

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:31.520
have on that.

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:33.520
And then, if you decide
that you want to reboot,

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:35.540
and you want to reshape
your VM, for example,

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:37.540
then you still have
everything persisting there.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:38.456
BRIAN DORSEY:
That's how it works,

00:11:38.456 --> 00:11:40.331
so you know what we're
talking about earlier.

00:11:40.331 --> 00:11:42.373
It's because that disk
lives on, past the config.

00:11:42.373 --> 00:11:43.956
STEPHANIE WONG: This
all sounds great,

00:11:43.956 --> 00:11:45.970
but are there any
gotchas or any caveats

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:46.870
here that we should be aware of?

00:11:46.870 --> 00:11:47.870
BRIAN DORSEY: Of course.

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.620
There's always trade-offs
in the world of tech.

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:54.040
And I think one of the things
that's not super obvious

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:58.035
is that everybody wants to use
disks differently in the cloud.

00:11:58.035 --> 00:11:59.410
Some people have
tons of storage.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.780
Some people want
really high throughput.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:03.730
And the way we balance
all of that out

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:08.740
is by actually connecting, like
on a real disk, the throughput

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.360
to the size of the disk.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:11.225
It's not obvious.

00:12:11.225 --> 00:12:13.600
We don't think about it that
way when we buy a real disk.

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:15.225
But there's a certain
amount of storage

00:12:15.225 --> 00:12:16.990
and a certain amount
of I/O that you

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:18.910
get when you buy one of them.

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:21.320
We extend that out through
the whole platform,

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:24.100
so one of the things
that can be a gotcha is--

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:28.047
you go to do a performance test.

00:12:28.047 --> 00:12:30.380
You pick a small disk, because
you're just doing a test,

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:31.730
and it doesn't work that well.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:33.730
And you're like, what?

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.430
This thing sucks.

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:38.140
It's important to
look at the docs

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:41.990
and pick the right-size disk
for your throughput target.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:45.580
The short version is that
larger disks are faster,

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:48.580
and they are a
network disk, so that

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.100
uses a certain amount
of the network, which

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:52.960
also uses some of the CPU.

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.170
The number of CPUs affects
the overall throughput,

00:12:57.170 --> 00:12:58.420
and the size of the disk does.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.170
So take a look at those
docs and make sure

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:02.950
that you size it right for the
workload you're trying to get.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:03.880
STEPHANIE WONG: Let's
talk about a scenario

00:13:03.880 --> 00:13:05.800
where you want to take
a snapshot of a VM

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:09.248
for disaster recovery or any
other use case related to that.

00:13:09.248 --> 00:13:10.540
Is a different on Google Cloud?

00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:13.040
Because I imagine you still
have to take a snapshot.

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:14.820
What can it enable
you to actually do?

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.153
BRIAN DORSEY: This is where
their being network-attached

00:13:17.153 --> 00:13:23.450
storage is huge, because we can
take a snapshot without hardly

00:13:23.450 --> 00:13:25.970
affecting the actual
usage of the disk at all,

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:28.580
and that snapshot can be used
to make a new disk anywhere

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:30.755
in the world, globally,
in the same system.

00:13:30.755 --> 00:13:32.630
If you're just trying
to do straight backups,

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.213
or you want to make
another copy of it

00:13:34.213 --> 00:13:37.410
somewhere else for reliability,
redundancy reasons,

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:38.660
that's really straightforward.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:41.077
Additionally, if you're trying
to build a full-on disaster

00:13:41.077 --> 00:13:43.610
recovery situation,
it might make sense

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:48.290
to think about the overall
system a little differently.

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:51.430
It may make sense to have
the database online and doing

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:54.400
a database-level replication,
maybe to a smaller instance,

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.620
and then the rest of the system
behind one of these autoscaling

00:13:57.620 --> 00:13:59.960
groups, where it only gets
bigger, to full size, when

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:00.930
it's actually running.

00:14:00.930 --> 00:14:04.325
This sort of thing is
something I've talked

00:14:04.325 --> 00:14:05.450
to a couple of folks about.

00:14:05.450 --> 00:14:07.700
They are thinking, oh, I
want to do disaster recovery

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:08.600
in the cloud.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:11.780
We have 25 machines running
the full thing on our side

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.730
or in another zone.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:16.160
We need 25 machines for
the disaster recovery site,

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.230
and it's the obvious,
straightforward way

00:14:18.230 --> 00:14:20.713
to think about it, model it.

00:14:20.713 --> 00:14:22.130
But likely, you
probably only need

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:24.350
one or two machines
running all the time,

00:14:24.350 --> 00:14:25.670
and the rest of them--

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.590
you can take advantage
of the flexibility

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:30.850
and just make them exist when
you actually have a disaster.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:32.600
STEPHANIE WONG: And
if that were to occur,

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.150
I'd imagine that there is a
pretty fast ability for it

00:14:35.150 --> 00:14:36.530
to become active?

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:37.310
BRIAN DORSEY: Yep.

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:39.643
It's the same thing we were
talking about earlier, where

00:14:39.643 --> 00:14:41.840
the individual machines
are up in 20, 30 seconds,

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:43.590
and they're behind the
same load balancer,

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:45.715
so they just scale up to
deal with the new traffic.

00:14:45.715 --> 00:14:48.007
STEPHANIE WONG: We talked a
lot about a lot of things--

00:14:48.007 --> 00:14:48.830
VMs.

00:14:48.830 --> 00:14:53.690
We talked about load balancers,
disks, rethinking VMs entirely.

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:56.062
What can people do to get
value out of Cloud right now?

00:14:56.062 --> 00:14:57.770
BRIAN DORSEY: That's,
in fact, the reason

00:14:57.770 --> 00:14:59.810
I'm excited about
this, because VMs

00:14:59.810 --> 00:15:02.930
are almost always the
next step for people

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:04.880
who are getting into Cloud.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.020
It's closest to the
architectures they currently

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:07.520
have.

00:15:07.520 --> 00:15:09.200
You can run almost
any software there.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:10.500
So they're the next step.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.930
And if you're building
up that architecture,

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:15.290
it really helps to dig a
little deeper on these,

00:15:15.290 --> 00:15:18.530
because you can simplify or
maybe leave whole parts of it

00:15:18.530 --> 00:15:21.890
out and still get the same
reliability or more, and more

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:23.210
flexibility out of it.

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:26.870
That's where the saving
time and money comes in.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:29.150
I really encourage
people to go take a look

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:31.400
at the architectures
they're thinking about,

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:35.600
connect them to the features
we have to simplify them,

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:37.690
and see where you can
save some time and money.

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:38.690
STEPHANIE WONG: Awesome.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:40.653
Well, that's great
words to end on.

00:15:40.653 --> 00:15:43.070
And also, I want to thank you
for coming back on the show,

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:43.625
because I think you have--

00:15:43.625 --> 00:15:44.030
BRIAN DORSEY: Thank you.

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:45.613
STEPHANIE WONG: --so
much good insight

00:15:45.613 --> 00:15:47.300
into shifting your
mental model of how

00:15:47.300 --> 00:15:48.535
to think about Cloud and VMs.

00:15:48.535 --> 00:15:49.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:50.032
Thanks again.

00:15:50.032 --> 00:15:51.740
Everyone, again, please
join us next time

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:55.230
for "Eyes on Enterprise,"
and we'll see you again soon.

